---
layout: article
title: Automations
description: Learn how Appwrite Agent automations run scheduled prompts and create conversations on a cron schedule.
---

**Automations** run Agent prompts on a schedule. Each run creates a new conversation with your prompt, optional model, and project context so you can review the result later.

Manage automations from `/agent/automations` or the Agent automations panel in the Console.

# What an automation contains {% #what-an-automation-contains %}

| Field | Purpose |
|-------|---------|
| Name | Label in the automations list |
| Prompt | The message sent to the Agent on each run |
| Schedule | Cron expression (UI helpers cover common weekly and daily patterns) |
| Title prefix | Optional prefix for conversation titles created by the run |
| Model | Optional custom or default model for the run |
| Enabled | Whether the schedule is active |
| Context project | Optional project used as context when the automation runs |

# How runs work {% #how-runs-work %}

1. When the schedule fires, Appwrite creates a new conversation linked to the automation.
2. The Agent processes the prompt with the configured model and context.
3. You open the resulting conversation to read the output, tool activity, and any resource cards or charts.

Automation conversations are kept with the automation rather than mixed into the main Agents list, so scheduled work stays organized.

# Enabling and disabling {% #enabling-and-disabling %}

Disable an automation to pause the schedule without deleting it. Enable it again when you want runs to resume. Delete an automation when you no longer need the schedule.

# Example use cases {% #example-use-cases %}

- Weekly summary of Auth signups or error-prone areas to review
- Recurring checklist: verify storage buckets, function deployments, or domain status
- Scheduled “what changed?” review for a project you do not open every day

Prompts that need live project data work best when [Appwrite MCP](/docs/products/agent/mcp) stays connected for your account.

